\section{Common classes}{

\code{catchmentACS_message_water_tract_filter} announces ACS water or
special-purpose tracts dropped by \code{cacs_acs_prefetch()}.

\code{catchmentACS_message_progress_tick} and
\code{catchmentACS_message_progress_summary} report long-running phase progress.

\code{catchmentACS_message_res_default_changed} announces the grid resolution
(\code{res}) chosen for OSRM when you omit the \code{res} argument.

\code{catchmentACS_message_demo_budget_protected} announces that the omitted-\code{res}
OSRM default was downgraded from \code{70L} to \code{30L} when \code{osrm_mode = "demo"},
protecting against HTTP 429 rate limits on the public OSRM demo endpoint.
Suppress via \code{options(catchmentACS.osrm_demo_budget_protect = FALSE)}.

\code{catchmentACS_message_rate_first_changed} announces a row-order change when
coercing a run result to a tibble: derived rate rows surface above the
source ACS rows within each site block. Disable the rate-first lift via
\code{rate_first = FALSE} or
\code{options(catchmentACS.rate_first_default = FALSE)}.

\code{catchmentACS_message_listcol_iso_filled} announces that the
\code{cacs_run(output = "list_column")} \verb{$isochrone} column was populated from
resolved isochrones.

\code{catchmentACS_message_perf_fix_applied} announces that an OSRM endpoint is
using the fast-server path rather than the public demo server's
forced-sleep path.

\code{catchmentACS_message_resolve_site} announces that a visualization \verb{(lat, lon)} input was resolved to the nearest \code{site_id}.

\verb{catchmentACS_message_cache_*} and \verb{catchmentACS_warning_cache_*} describe
cache hits, legacy invalidation, fingerprint mismatch, cache enablement, and
stale-cache suspicion.

\code{catchmentACS_warning_rate_out_of_range} and
\code{catchmentACS_warning_carrier_missing} describe rate derivation and MOE
fallback concerns.

\code{catchmentACS_warning_resolve_site_distant} warns that a visualization
\verb{(lat, lon)} input resolved to a site more than 5 km away.

\code{catchmentACS_warning_geometry_skip} reports degenerate tract geometry that
was skipped while preserving the rest of an intersection.

\code{catchmentACS_warning_partial} reports partial isochrone failure rows carried
through by \code{cacs_run()}.

\code{catchmentACS_error_missing_suggest} reports a missing optional package for
visualization helpers.
}

\section{Backward compatibility}{

Two legacy error-class aliases are retained for handlers written against
early development builds. Annulus-input errors carry
\code{cacs_error_annulus_input} alongside the canonical
\code{catchmentACS_error_annulus_input}, and schema-family errors carry
\code{cacs_error_schema} alongside the canonical \code{catchmentACS_error_schema}.
New code should prefer the canonical \verb{catchmentACS_*} class names; the
legacy aliases are compatibility shims.
}

\section{Capturing conditions}{

Use \code{\link[=cacs_capture_conditions]{cacs_capture_conditions()}} for the common case:

\preformatted{
conds <- cacs_capture_conditions(
  cacs_run(sites = sites, state = "AL", verbose = TRUE)
)

water <- cacs_capture_conditions(
  cacs_acs_prefetch(state = "AL", verbose = TRUE),
  classes = "water_tract_filter"
)
}

To write custom handlers directly, subscribe to a leaf class or to a parent
class:

\preformatted{
withCallingHandlers(
  cacs_run(sites = sites, state = "AL"),
  catchmentACS_message_progress = function(m) {
    # progress messages
    invokeRestart("muffleMessage")
  },
  catchmentACS_warning = function(w) {
    # warnings
    invokeRestart("muffleWarning")
  }
)
}
}

\section{Muting}{

\code{suppressMessages()} mutes message conditions and \code{suppressWarnings()} mutes
warning conditions. Prefer \code{\link[=cacs_capture_conditions]{cacs_capture_conditions()}} when you need an audit
table rather than complete silence.
}

